    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d1200">

                <pb facs="1309" n="1299" sameAs="frus:frus1944v04/pg1299"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">740.0011 European War 1939/34041: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to the Minister in
                        Switzerland</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Harrison</hi>)</head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Washington</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1944-05-04T13:00:00-04:00">May 4, 1944—1
                            p.m.</date>
                    </dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>1550. For Tittmann. Your 131, April 13—Bern’s 2580, April 22. With the
                    concurrence of the War Department you may in your discretion acknowledge the
                    Vatican’s memorandum of April 12 repeating that the Allied Commanders in the
                    field have unquestionable evidence that the Abbey of Monte Cassino formed part
                    of the German defensive system.<anchor n="35" xml:id="d1200-f1"/> We have no
                    further comments to make at this time.</p>
                <p>Sent to Bern, repeated to Algiers.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Hull</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d1200-f1" n="35" xml:id="d1200-f1-content">
                <p>For a discussion of this subject, see Martin Blumenson, <hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Salerno to Cassino</hi>, in the official Army history <hi
                        rendition="#italic">United States Army in World War II</hi> (in
                    preparation).</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
